+
+# WelcomePi updates
+
+Kiosk operators can enroll a working entrance display in automatic updates. Complete a local fob tap and a door check-in first. On the Pi, run `sudo bash setup.sh enable-updates` from `~/WelcomePi`. The Pi checks for a tested release about every five minutes. [Pi Zero W setup and recovery guide](https://github.com/ManchesterMakerspace/makerspace-rails-2026/blob/master/tools/WelcomePi/setup-pi0.MD#8-enable-automatic-updates-from-master)
+
+Run `systemctl list-timers welcomepi-update.timer` to see the next check. To check immediately, run `sudo systemctl start welcomepi-update.service`. A new app release briefly restarts the display and returns it to the generic board. The kiosk keeps running through a failed download and restores its previous version after a failed startup check.
+
+For an update problem, inspect `sudo journalctl -b -u welcomepi-update.service`. The setup guide covers held releases and pausing updates for host maintenance. After a successful update, confirm one welcome at the kiosk reader and another from a real door check-in.
+
+## Reader and scan logs
+
+After a restart, look for the PN532 provisioning entry in `sudo journalctl -b -t welcomepi` or on the kiosk's configured remote syslog collector. The kiosk tries I²C, then UART. If neither responds, door check-ins still operate the display. Each scan entry identifies the reader and, when verified, the member. A warning marks a scan with no valid member. MQTT and MongoDB errors include the driver reason. Use these records to investigate a reported scan at the time and door the member gives you.
